# What this shared library exports, and why the list is this short.
#
# Statically linking aws-lc puts a complete BoringSSL inside this object. With
# no version script the linker exports all of it: a measurement on the first
# build that linked it found 1,700 exported symbols, of which 1,684 were the
# crypto library's internals -- 304 EVP_*, 97 EC_*, 94 BIO_*, 80 RSA_*.
#
# That is not untidiness. This process already runs a second BoringSSL:
# Conscrypt, which is what the L1 TLS path uses for its RFC 5705 exporter and
# for ALPN. Two BoringSSLs in one process, both exporting the same names
# globally, is the configuration in which a symbol resolves into the wrong one
# -- and the damage would land inside TLS, silently, a long way from its cause.
#
# So the library exports its JNI entry points and nothing else. Everything the
# C side needs from the JVM it gets by calling in, not by being called by name.
# JNI_OnLoad and JNI_OnUnload are deliberately absent rather than listed "in
# case". This library registers natively by name, defines neither, and the NDK
# links with --no-undefined-version, so naming a symbol that does not exist is
# a link error rather than a harmless extra line. That is the right trade: this
# file cannot quietly drift into describing a library it no longer matches.
{
    global:
        Java_*;
    local:
        *;
};
